<%# The part of the requester form that depends on the answers so far, and
    therefore has to be re-rendered when one of them changes (PRD M11).

    A Turbo frame, which is the modern equivalent of the `#issue-dependent-fields`
    container Easy8's own new-issue form swaps: the requester answers a field
    some section's rule reads, the page posts every answer back to
    EasyFormDesignerSubmissionsController#refresh, and the server re-renders
    exactly this partial with the section rules evaluated against those
    answers. The rule itself is never evaluated client-side — RuleEvaluator
    stays the only implementation, and the browser only asks for a new render.

    Everything that does NOT depend on the answers (the form's description,
    the flash) deliberately stays outside the frame, the same way Easy8 keeps
    subject and description outside the swapped region of the issue form. %>
<%= turbo_frame_tag EasyFormDesignerSubmissionsController::FIELDS_FRAME_ID do %>
  <%# REQ-16. Every field lives in a section now, so rendering is a real
      nested walk — sections outer, that section's own fields inner —
      rather than a flat field list with a heading injected whenever the
      section happened to change. A section with no renderable field left
      in it (every field hidden, or the whole section itself gated shut)
      renders nothing at all: no orphan heading.

      Two different reasons a field renders NO input at all here — and in
      both cases the absence IS the security boundary, not a cosmetic hide,
      because the server discards anything a crafted request sends for such
      a token anyway (see AnswerResolver):

      * PRD M8 — `hidden?`, whose value comes from the author's preset.
      * PRD M11 — the section itself has a visibility rule that evaluates
        false for the answers so far (@form.visible_sections). %>
  <% @form.visible_sections(@answers).each do |section| %>
    <% renderable_fields = section.fields.reject(&:hidden?) %>
    <% next if renderable_fields.empty? %>

    <h3 class="efd-form-section" data-cy="section__group--<%= section.token %>"><%= section.name %></h3>

    <% renderable_fields.each do |field| %>
    <% name = "answers[#{field.token}]" %>
    <% value = @answers[field.token] %>
    <% field_messages = Array(@field_errors && @field_errors[field.token]) %>

    <% # Only DSPasswordField supports helper_text natively — every other DS
       # form control here does not, so field.help_text is rendered as a
       # plain caption instead of being passed through as a keyword. %>
    <% case field.widget %>
    <% when "long_text" %>
      <%= ds_textarea(name: name, value: value, label: field.label,
                      required: field.required?, rows: 4) %>
    <% when "select" %>
      <%# Plain native select, not ds_select — see the note in
          easy_form_designer_forms/new.html.erb: <easy8-ds-select> crashes
          its own internal Vue mount client-side regardless of wrapping
          context, leaving the dropdown permanently empty. A requester
          hitting that on a real Dropdown-mapped field would be unable to
          submit at all, so this isn't a cosmetic downgrade.

          It is also the only shape that works as a rule trigger: DSSelect
          submits through a hidden input, and Vue writing .value on a hidden
          input dispatches no `change` event at all. %>
      <div class="form-group">
        <label for="field_<%= field.token %>">
          <%= field.label %><%= " *".html_safe if field.required? %>
        </label>
        <%# include_blank is unconditional — a required field must still
            start with nothing chosen. `include_blank: !field.required?`
            here previously meant a required select had no blank option,
            so the browser auto-selected the first real option (e.g.
            "Low" priority) with no requester action at all: a silently
            guessed answer, not a validated one. required: still applies
            the native HTML — and our own — required check to the blank
            option, so an unanswered required select is now caught rather
            than defaulted. %>
        <%= select_tag name,
                      options_for_select(field.options, value),
                      include_blank: true, required: field.required?,
                      id: "field_#{field.token}" %>
      </div>
    <% when "number" %>
      <%# The author's M7 range, when set, wins over the implicit floor.
          min: 0 remains the fallback for the one native attribute with a
          known-safe floor (estimated hours can't be negative) — dropping it
          when no range is authored would be a regression. The server
          re-checks both bounds regardless; these attributes only save the
          requester a round trip. %>
      <%# format_bound, not the raw BigDecimal: these land in the component's
          data attributes via to_s, and BigDecimal#to_s emits scientific
          notation — a maximum of 40 would reach the browser as min="0.4e2",
          which it ignores, silently dropping the client-side bound. %>
      <% min_bound = EasyFormDesigner::FormField.format_bound(field.min_value) %>
      <%= ds_number_field(name: name, value: value, label: field.label,
                          required: field.required?,
                          min: min_bound || (0 if field.mapped_attribute == "estimated_hours"),
                          max: EasyFormDesigner::FormField.format_bound(field.max_value)) %>
    <% when "radio" %>
      <%# Plain radio_button_tag, not ds_radio_group — DSRadioGroup's
          "options" prop never hydrates from the server-rendered
          <script type="application/json"> prop tag outside the SPA
          mount context (confirmed live: Vue logs "Missing required
          prop: options" and the fieldset renders with no inputs at
          all), the same class of standalone-mount limitation already
          documented above for <easy8-ds-select>. %>
      <fieldset class="form-group">
        <legend><%= field.label %><%= " *".html_safe if field.required? %></legend>
        <% field.options.each do |option_label, option_value| %>
          <label>
            <%= radio_button_tag name, option_value, value == option_value,
                                 required: field.required? %>
            <%= option_label %>
          </label>
        <% end %>
      </fieldset>
    <% when "multi_select" %>
      <%# Same reasoning as the "select" case above: a multi-value select
          has the identical <easy8-ds-select> mount bug, so it inherits the
          identical plain select_tag fallback — here with multiple: true
          and an array-shaped param name. %>
      <div class="form-group">
        <label for="field_<%= field.token %>">
          <%= field.label %><%= " *".html_safe if field.required? %>
        </label>
        <%= select_tag "#{name}[]",
                      options_for_select(field.options, Array(value)),
                      multiple: true, required: field.required?,
                      id: "field_#{field.token}" %>
      </div>
    <% when "checkbox" %>
      <%# Plain check_box_tag, not DesignSystem::Components::Checkbox —
          its "option" prop has the same standalone-mount hydration
          failure described above for RadioGroup (confirmed live: Vue
          logs "Missing required prop: option" and nothing renders).
          hidden_field_tag first is the standard Rails idiom so an
          unchecked box still submits "0" rather than nothing at all —
          without it, missing?/answerable? below would see no param and
          treat "explicitly unchecked" as "never answered" again, the
          exact bug this widget exists to fix. %>
      <%# id: nil — hidden_field_tag and check_box_tag otherwise both
          default their id from the same name, which is invalid HTML
          (duplicate ids) and made Playwright resolve the wrong element
          during verification. %>
      <%= hidden_field_tag name, "0", id: nil %>
      <label>
        <%= check_box_tag name, "1", value == "1" %>
        <%= field.label %><%= " *".html_safe if field.required? %>
      </label>
    <% when "user" %>
      <%# No bare ds_autocomplete helper exists in core, so the component is
          called directly — same reasoning as Checkbox above. Scoped to this
          form's own project via collection_args, the same mechanism core
          itself uses to scope a principal autocomplete to one project's
          members rather than the whole instance. %>
      <%= DesignSystem::Components::Autocomplete.render(
            view_context: self, entity: "principal", name: name,
            label: field.label, required: field.required?,
            clearable: !field.required?,
            settings: { collection: "visible_active_users",
                        collection_args: { project_id: @form.project.id } },
            value: existing_principal_value(value)
          ) %>
    <% when "date" %>
      <%# DesignSystem::Components::Datepicker has no required: keyword at
          all (confirmed from its initialize signature) — passing one
          raises ArgumentError unconditionally. label: is omitted and
          replaced with a manual <label> so the required marker matches
          every other field type's plain asterisk instead of a separate
          "Required" line — Datepicker manages its own internal id/label
          pairing regardless of whether label: is passed, so nothing is
          lost by supplying our own here. Required-ness is still enforced
          server-side by missing_required_fields regardless of this.

          An authored date-range rule (SubmissionValidator) has NO
          client-side counterpart here, unlike email/URL/number — the
          component takes no min/max keyword at all, so there is nothing to
          pass one through to. The requester only learns about it from the
          server-side error after submitting; that is the correct behaviour
          for this widget today, not a shortcut taken under time
          pressure. %>
      <label>
        <%= field.label %><%= " *".html_safe if field.required? %>
      </label>
      <%= ds_datepicker(name: name, value: value, label: nil) %>
    <% when "file" %>
      <%# PRD M13. A plain file_field_tag, not DSFileUpload — the DS
          component is Vue-only (there is no ds_file_upload helper and no
          server-side FileUpload wrapper class), and every other complex DS
          component tried in this view failed to hydrate when mounted
          standalone outside the SPA shell (see the select/radio/checkbox/
          autocomplete notes above). No reason to expect a fifth one to
          behave differently.

          No value: — browsers refuse to pre-populate a file input, for
          obvious security reasons. A refresh of this frame would therefore
          lose an already-picked file, which is why the refresh script moves
          the live input element across instead of letting Turbo replace it
          (see submission_form.ts). It is a plain input precisely because
          that transplant is safe — a DS custom element must never be
          re-inserted, its prop <script> children are consumed on first mount.

          The [] suffix is what makes Rails collect several uploads into
          one array under this field's token, which is what
          IssueBuilder#attach_files iterates. %>
      <label>
        <%= field.label %><%= " *".html_safe if field.required? %>
      </label>
      <%= file_field_tag "#{name}[]", multiple: true, id: "field_#{field.token}" %>
    <% else %>
      <%# An M7 email/URL rule also drives the input type, so the browser
          checks before a round trip. TextField#initialize does accept type:.
          The server is still the authority — see SubmissionValidator, whose
          URL rule requires the same explicit scheme type="url" demands, so
          the two never disagree about the same value. %>
      <%= ds_text_field(name: name, value: value, label: field.label,
                        required: field.required?,
                        type: EasyFormDesigner::FormField::HTML_INPUT_TYPES[field.validation_format]) %>
    <% end %>

    <% if field.help_text.present? %>
      <p class="caption"><%= field.help_text %></p>
    <% end %>

    <%# Every message SubmissionValidator (or the rejected Issue) produced for
        this specific field. Previously a single hardcoded "cannot be blank",
        which was the only failure that could be attributed to a field at
        all — every format or custom-field rule ended up in the flash. %>
    <% field_messages.each do |message| %>
      <span class="ds-form-field__error"><%= message %></span>
    <% end %>
    <% end %>
  <% end %>
<% end %>
